On Thu, 17 Aug 2006, Topi Linkala wrote:
>
> I've restarted my annotated NetHack code project and one of the problems is 
> that what we are calling items in NetHack are called objects in the code. 
> These objects in the code contain also such objects as venoms, unpaid used 
> items and items that are currently in use, whisch are not items per se.
>
> So my question is: What should I call these in my annotations: objects or 
> items?

   I pretty much agree with Dylan's comment. The best solution I can think
of would be to use the source code's names as much as possible in your
annotations, so that the reader gets comfortable with the code's names
and will later be able to understand the code even without your
annotations. But then have a "glossary" or foreword or something in which
you explain the rough mapping between the source code/annotation names
and the names used by normal people. :)

> The code also has an array objects[] which doesn't contain any objects
> at all but the object types.

   Good example: You'll obviously have to distinguish between "objects",
"object types", and "object classes", even though regular players don't
talk about Nethack's "object classes". So your annotations will have to
deal with the source-code names in this case --- you can't use the 
normal-people names, because there aren't any.

> So what would you suggest? All items, all objects or depending on
> what the code is doing?

   Depending on what the code is doing, plus copious cross-references
("This function takes an item, or what blargle_object() calls an 
object...") and a glossary explaining as many source-code names as
possible.

-Arthur


